<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mir Damad</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1561–<a href="/years/1631">1631</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Mīr Dāmād, Mir Mohammad Baqer Astarabadi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mir Damad, born Seyyed Mir Mohammad Baqer Astarabadi, was an Iranian Twelver Shia philosopher in the tradition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avicenna">Avicenna</a>, who died in 1631 or 1632. With his student Mulla Sadra he was the foremost figure of the cultural renaissance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid dynasty</a>, and he is regarded as the founder of the School of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>; admirers called him the Third Teacher after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> and al-Farabi. His chief idea was atemporal origination, which sought to resolve whether the world was created in time. His name, Groom of the King, came from his marriage to a daughter of Shah Abbas. He wrote 134 known works, some of them poetry under the pen name Ishraq.</p></div>
